<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?>
<!-- =====================================================================
     PRJEDU_snmp - AyMON example: SNMP, both ends, nothing to install.

     One engine, two documents, real SNMP packets on a loopback socket:

       AGT/S00   the device. An SNMP v2c AGENT, written in script on top of
                 PRO.raw, serving a handful of OIDs from a simulated tank.
       MON/S00   the monitoring side. AyMON in the role it really has:
                 an SNMP MANAGER polling those OIDs into <io> points with
                 units, scaling and alarms.

     WHY THE AGENT IS SCRIPT. AyMON's PRO.snmp is a manager only - it asks,
     it does not answer. The other examples put both ends in one engine
     with two built-in roles (PRJEDU_modbus) or one built-in broker
     (PRJEDU_mqtt); SNMP has no such second role, so the device end is
     built from the three pieces the engine gives a script for exactly
     this purpose: PRO.raw (a connection with no protocol), pro_onparse
     (the framing hook) and CODEC.HEX_dec / HEX_enc (bytes <-> text).
     It needs engine 4.3.4.444 or later.

     Read db_MON.xml if you came for SNMP: that file is what you would
     write for a real device. Read db_AGT.js if you ever have to speak a
     protocol AyMON does not know yet.

     Run:  run.bat
     ===================================================================== -->
<xdb id="XDB" xdb_lib="AyPRO" xdb_log="4" log_cfg="0x39C0" log_lnk="%PRJ%/log/XDB.log"
     sta="0x0300" tid="S01">
	<config>
		<doc id="AGT/S00" sta="0x0300" src="%DB%/db_AGT.xml" desc="the device - an SNMP agent in script" />
		<doc id="MON/S00" sta="0x0300" src="%DB%/db_MON.xml" desc="the monitoring side - SNMP manager" />
	</config>
	<data>
	</data>
</xdb>
